        <li><a href="#sec-8">What to photograph and keep</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Why two riders change the paperwork questions</h2>
    <p>A solo rider reads a rental slip and sees their own name, their own deposit and their own responsibilities. A pair reads the same slip and should immediately ask the questions the slip was never forced to answer: who may ride, who is covered if the passenger takes a turn, and what happens to the deposit if the second helmet goes missing. None of these are exotic; they are simply the gaps that appear the moment the machine carries two people instead of one.</p>
    <p>The honest approach is to treat paperwork as a conversation with a record, not a formality with a signature. Every question asked out loud at the counter becomes a sentence on the slip or a note on the receipt, and every question skipped becomes a negotiation at return time, held at a counter, possibly in a rush. Pairs who ask first ride with fewer open questions all week.</p>
    <p>There is also a fairness reading. The passenger signs nothing and carries no obligations, which means the paperwork burden sits entirely with the rider. A pair that treats the paperwork as shared work, the passenger listening, photographing and remembering, ends the rental with the same clean hand-back as the one that started it, and the questions in this page are built to be asked together.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">The documents the counter records</h2>
    <p>Every rental records who takes the machine, and the practical questions are about what the counter needs and what it does with it. Ask plainly: which document is recorded, whether the original stays at the shop or a copy or photograph suffices, and how the document is returned at hand-back. Shops answer these questions every day, and asking them is a sign of a careful renter rather than a suspicious one.</p>
    <p>The pair should also ask what the record includes about the rental itself: the machine, the day rate, the start date and the agreed return day. The published prices for this fleet are straightforward, 150,000 VND per day for the Honda Wave, Yamaha Sirius, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click, and 200,000 VND per day for the Honda Vision, Honda Air Blade, the electric motorbike and the 50cc tier, all listed on the cost hub, and the slip should reflect the same numbers.</p>
    <p>The passenger documents are the quiet follow-up: ask whether the counter needs anything from the second rider at all. Most rentals record one rider, and knowing that in advance settles who holds which documents during the trip, who carries the spare copy and who the shop will call if the return runs late. Small answers, but they belong on paper rather than in memory.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Questions about the second helmet and accessories</h2>
    <p>The single most important two-up question is the helmet one: does the rental include a second helmet for the passenger, what condition is it in, and is its fit worth checking the same way the rider checks their own. The answer shapes the first five minutes of riding more than any other line on the slip, and it should be asked while the passenger is standing there, not after they are waiting at the curb.</p>
    <p>Accessories follow the same logic. Ask what else comes with this specific machine: a rain cover, a phone holder, a top box, a lock. Each answer is shop-specific equipment for this rental rather than a fleet guarantee, so the slip or the receipt should note whatever is agreed, because the hand-back will count the same items back in. A pair that skips this list pays for it in a fumbled return.</p>
    <p>The third helmet question belongs to multi-day renters: what happens if a helmet is damaged or lost mid-rental. Shops answer this calmly every week, and the answer belongs on the slip or at least in a photographed note, because a lost item dispute at return time is the most common paperwork friction in the entire category, and the cheapest one to prevent.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Deposits, holds and what secures what</h2>
    <p>Deposit practices vary from shop to shop, which is precisely why the deposit questions belong at the counter rather than in assumption. Ask what the deposit is, what it secures, when it comes back, and what would hold part of it back. A shop that answers all four questions in one breath is giving the pair the entire deposit policy in usable form, and the answers should be noted the same way the condition is.</p>
    <p>The condition and the deposit are two ends of the same conversation, and the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/motorbike-rental-deposit-hanoi/">deposit questions guide</a> covers the general category in depth for every audience. For a pair the addition is simple: the slip should reflect the machine as it stands, and both riders should have seen the same condition the slip describes, so the deposit conversation starts from shared facts rather than a solo memory.</p>
    <p>One habit protects every deposit: photograph the machine in its starting condition, four sides plus the odometer, and photograph the slip itself. Ninety seconds of photos settles more return-day questions than any other single act in the rental, and it costs nothing to do while the counter fills in the paperwork in parallel.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Reading the condition slip as a pair</h2>
    <p>The condition slip is the record of what the machine already carries: scratches, dents, worn panels, the small history of every previous renter. Read it with the same eyes as the bike itself, and read it together, because the passenger will see the machine from angles the rider never sits behind. Existing damage that is not on the slip should be written onto it before signing, and most counters hand the pen over without being asked twice.</p>
    <p>The slip also tells the truth about the machine tier. A pair that rented expecting a scooter and finds a semi-automatic on the slip has caught the mismatch at the cheapest possible moment. Model, color and plate number should match what is standing in front of both riders, and any mismatch is a question for the counter rather than a compromise for later.</p>
    <p>The pre-ride side of the same conversation lives in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-a-passenger-pre-ride-inspection/">pre-ride inspection chapter</a>, which covers tires, brakes, controls and the five-minute routine. The paperwork and the inspection are the same habit done twice: once in writing, once with hands, and a pair that does both hands the machine back the way they received it.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Rental terms worth asking out loud</h2>
    <p>Terms are the sentences nobody reads until they matter. The honest ones for a pair: when does the rental day end, what happens if the return slips by a few hours, and what the shop expects to hear if plans change mid-week. Shops deal with real schedules all year, and the answers are usually calmer than renters fear, but they are answers the pair should have in writing or in a photographed note rather than as a guess.</p>
    <p>The insurance word deserves a plain question too: what does the rental cover if the machine is damaged, and what does it not. The answer is shop policy, and this page does not invent one; the job of the pair is to ask the question, hear the specific answer for this rental, and let it shape how carefully the week is ridden. The <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/hanoi-motorbike-traffic-rules/">traffic rules guide</a> covers the legal backdrop for riding in Hanoi.</p>
    <p>The last term is the pair-specific one: whether the passenger may take a turn riding. If the plan includes swapping riders at any point, the paperwork should say so, because a machine handed to an unrecorded rider is the quietest way to turn a small incident into a paperwork problem. Ask, note the answer, and let it settle who rides and who rides pillion for the whole rental.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">Licensing questions for whoever rides</h2>
    <p>Licensing is the one question the paperwork cannot answer creatively: whoever rides needs to be properly licensed for what they ride, and the honest source is official rather than anecdotal. The <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/motorbike-license-vietnam-tourists/">license guide for tourists</a> covers the categories, the international driving permit question and what applies to visitors, and every rider should read it before the counter conversation rather than after.</p>
    <p>At the counter the licensing questions are short: does the shop record license details with the rental, and does anything in the agreement depend on the rider holding a specific category. The answers connect the paperwork to the rider real situation, and a pair where both riders might take the handlebars should ask the question for both people, not just the one who happens to be holding the pen.</p>
    <p>The reason this section belongs in the paperwork chapter rather than only in a riding guide is practical: licensing questions answered at the counter become part of the agreement, while licensing questions answered later become part of a dispute. Ten seconds of questions here make the whole rental cleaner, and the pair that asks them rides with one less thing to remember.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-8">What to photograph and keep</h2>
    <p>The photograph list is short: the machine from four sides, the odometer, any existing damage close up, the slip itself, and the shop details, 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open 09:00 to 21:00 daily. Taken together they are the entire rental agreement in pictures, and ninety seconds at the counter replaces an hour of memory at the return.</p>
    <p>Keep the paperwork reachable for the whole rental: the receipt or slip in a phone wallet or a photographed copy that survives a dead battery. The rider carries the originals, and the passenger carries the photographed set, which is the smallest possible backup system and the one that has never once been improved upon.</p>
    <p>The paperwork chapter of the luggage audience, on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-luggage-rental-paperwork-questions/">riders with luggage paperwork page</a>, asks the same questions from the solo-with-load angle, and pairs who read both will notice the overlap: the questions are the same because the agreement is the same, and only the helmet count changes.</p>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions about rental paperwork for two riders in Hanoi</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>What documents does the counter need from a pair renting one bike?</h2>
      <p>The rental records the rider who takes the machine, and the exact document requirement is shop policy, so the honest answer is the question itself: ask what is recorded, whether a copy or photograph suffices, and how it returns at hand-back. The pair should also ask whether the counter needs anything from the passenger at all, which settles who carries what for the whole rental.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Should the passenger be named on the rental paperwork?</h2>
      <p>Ask at the counter and let the answer land on the slip. Most rentals record one rider per machine, and if the pair plans to swap riders at any point, the paperwork should say so, because an unrecorded rider is the fastest way to turn a small incident into a paperwork problem. The question takes ten seconds at pickup and saves the entire negotiation later.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>What should we photograph before signing anything?</h2>
      <p>Four sides of the machine, the odometer, any existing damage close up, the condition slip itself, and the shop details. Ninety seconds of photos on one phone, with the passenger holding the second copy, settles nearly every return-day question before it exists, and it protects the shop as much as the pair.</p>
    </article>
